\nContrasting with the Gamma-Gaussian model typically employed in classical sparse Bayesian learning, we select the Bernoulli–Gaussian model as the hierarchical prior and infer a pruning condition for a single specular component within the SAGE framework.\n\n  * •\n\nMeanwhile, two novel joint estimation algorithms are proposed within this framework:\n\n  * •\n\n(1) optimizing the alternating iterative process inherent in the variational solving approach, while jointly optimizing a portion of the dispersion parameters and amplitude gain to enhance model order estimation without adding to the computational complexity;\n\n  * •\n\n(2) additionally, introducing a time-delay estimation computation method based on the autocorrelation characteristics of the sounding sequence, aimed at reducing algorithm complexity and speeding up convergence.\n\n  * •\n\nFinally, the performance advantages of the proposed algorithm are validated through simulations and measured data.\n\n  * •\n\nComparisons with related algorithms demonstrate that the proposed algorithm effectively accomplishes joint estimation of model order and channel parameters. Particularly, it achieves more accurate estimation of model order and dispersion parameters in scenarios with high signal-to-noise ratios.\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this paper, we jointly estimate the model order, amplitude gain and dispersion parameters of the received signal vector using a variational sparse Bayesian framework. Contrasting with the Gamma-Gaussian model typically employed in classical sparse Bayesian learning, we select the Bernoulli–Gaussian model as the hierarchical prior and infer a pruning condition for a single specular component within the SAGE framework. The adaptive thresholds derived from this approach are better suited to varying signal-to-noise ratios and provide improved model order estimation. Moreover, two novel joint estimation algorithms are proposed within this framework: (1) optimizing the alternating iterative process inherent in the variational solving approach, while jointly optimizing a portion of the dispersion parameters and amplitude gain to enhance model order estimation without adding to the computational complexity; (2) additionally, introducing a time-delay estimation computation method based on the autocorrelation characteristics of the sounding sequence, aimed at reducing algorithm complexity and speeding up convergence. Finally, the performance advantages of the proposed algorithm are validated through simulations and measured data. Comparisons with related algorithms demonstrate that the proposed algorithm effectively accomplishes joint estimation of model order and channel parameters. Particularly, it achieves more accurate estimation of model order and dispersion parameters in scenarios with high signal-to-noise ratios.\n\n## Keywords\n\nVariational Bayesian inference\n\n;\n\nChannel parameter estimation\n\n;\n\nBernoulli–Gaussian\n\n;\n\nSAGE\n\n## Introduction\n\nWith the emergence of ultra 5G and 6G technologies, wireless communications are moving toward larger bandwidth, lower latency, and higher transmission rates [1]. Multiple-Input Multiple-Output (MIMO) as a key technology improves system capacity, spatial diversity gain and spectrum utilization by increasing the spatial dimensions [2], but it also leads to challenging channel parameter estimation (CPE) problems for complex signal models.\n\nIt is of practical significance for the optimization of wireless communication system design to accurately estimate the dispersion parameters from received signal vector and establish a suitable channel model. The dispersion parameters typically include time delay, angle of arrival (AoA), angle of departure (AoD), and Doppler frequency. Despite extensive research, the accuracy and complexity of CPE algorithms continue to pose significant challenges that require further investigation.\n\nIn CPE algorithms, the received signal vector is usually modeled as a superposition of specular component (SC), and each SC can be viewed as the product of amplitude gain and the signal comprising its dispersion parameters. In this process, the components resulting from diffuse reflections are often excluded from consideration (also referred to as dense multipath component, or DMC), and their parameters can be estimated from the residual signal obtained by subtracting the estimated SC from the received signal vector [3]. However, in this paper we focus on the precise estimation of the SC, since the main energy of the received signal vector is concentrated in SCs, and the estimation of the diffuse component requires the accurate estimation of the SC in the first place.\n\nThe number of SCs determines the model order of the received signal vector. Under the condition that the model order is fixed, spectral estimation algorithms and subspace-based algorithms can efficiently estimate several of the dispersion parameters [4], [5], [6], [7], while algorithms based on the maximum likelihood (ML) criterion, such as the Expectation–Maximization (EM) and space alternating generalized expectation (SAGE) algorithms, exhibit superior accuracy [8], [9]. However, all these algorithms depend on prior information regarding the model order, and an incorrect choice will significantly reduce algorithm accuracy. Specifically, selecting a model order that is too high can result in the estimation of pseudo components (non-existent SCs and their dispersion parameters), while choosing lower model orders can lead to the omission of SCs.\n\nTherefore, it is essential to incorporate an algorithm for estimating model order into the CPE algorithm to enhance its practical applications. Traditional methods typically determine the model order using criteria such as the Akaike Information Criterion (AIC), Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC), Minimum Description Length (MDL), and others [10].However, these methods not only increase the computational complexity of the algorithm but also tend to introduce a positive bias in estimating the model order under non-asymptotic signal-to-noise-ratio (SNR) and the number of observed samples [11]. In the case of BIC, for example, the optimal model order needs to be selected from multiple candidate model orders based on likelihood penalties, which requires estimating dispersion parameters for each candidate, thereby introducing additional computational overhead.\n\nMany current studies are inspired by sparse Bayesian learning (SBL), which enables the joint estimation of model order and channel parameters by introducing prior distribution constraints on sparse parameters [12], [13], [14], [15], [16], [17], [18]. Ref. [12] introduced a super-resolution algorithm within the SBL framework for jointly estimating model order, time delay, and AoA. The benefits of using the SBL framework are twofold: (1) the estimation of the model order and other parameters are done in the same framework, avoiding unnecessary computational overheads; (2) the threshold for adjusting the number of SCs is derived from the prior distribution of the sparsity parameter, enabling iterative adjustment of SCs to adapt to time-varying scenarios.\n\nIn the SBL framework, the goal is to compute the posterior distribution of parameters given the observations. However, the process involves matrix inversion in each iteration, which is not feasible for larger-scale matrices. An alternative approach to SBL is using variational approximation for Bayesian inference [13], also known as sparse variational Bayesian inference (VBI). VBI typically decomposes the variational approximate distribution using mean field theory and employs an alternating iterative process to solve for the posterior distribution of parameters, thereby avoiding the computationally intensive inversion of large-scale matrices. Compared to SBL, VBI not only can approximate the probability density functions (PDFs) of unobserved variables but also provide approximate solution when estimating posterior distribution is challenging. Therefore, our research focuses on algorithms for the joint estimation of model orders based on VBI. Among the current state-of-the-art methods, Ref. [14] integrates the VBI framework with the SAGE algorithm, using it to achieve joint estimation of the number of SCs (model order) and dispersion parameters. Ref. [15] similarly combines VBI with the SAGE algorithm to bypass the matrix inversion step necessary in traditional SBL, thereby reducing the computational complexity of the algorithm. However, it does not address the estimation of dispersion parameters in the wireless channel. Refs. [16], [17] further consider the correlation between sparse parameters based on [14] to obtain a more general VBI process. However, this would increase the complexity of solving the model, so the correlation between the sparsity parameters is not considered in this paper. All of the above studies introduced Gamma-Gaussian hierarchical prior or Gamma-Laplace hierarchical prior in the VBI framework and did not consider other forms of prior distributions. Ref. [18] utilizes a Bernoulli–Gaussian hierarchical prior for jointly estimating model order and frequency parameters in line spectra. The analysis indicates that this hierarchical prior is more robust in detecting pseudo components. However, it does not extend this hierarchical prior to address the problem of channel parameter estimation, particularly when multidimensional dispersion parameters are included.\n\nIn this paper, we propose two improved algorithms within a variational Bayesian framework for jointly detecting and estimating multipath signal parameters. These algorithms effectively achieve simultaneous estimation of model order, amplitude gain, and dispersion parameters in a Single-Input Multiple-Output (SIMO) sounding system. In contrast to the related algorithm [14], we employ a Bernoulli–Gaussian hierarchical prior to improve the algorithm’s capability to identify pseudo components. We also derive the expression for the model pruning condition under the SAGE framework integrated with Bernoulli–Gaussian hierarchical prior, enhancing the accuracy of model order estimation. Secondly, we simultaneously optimize the amplitude gain and dispersion parameters throughout the iterative process to avoid local optima and enhance the accuracy of their estimation, all without increasing algorithm complexity. This approach is designated as Algorithm 1 in this study.\n\nBuilding upon the aforementioned improved algorithm, we pre-estimate the time delay parameters based on the good autocorrelation characteristics of the sounding sequence and derive the corresponding computational expressions. This approach reduces the search space for time delay parameters during estimation and accelerates the algorithm’s convergence speed. This method is referred to as Algorithm 2 in this study. Building on this, we validated the joint estimation performance of the algorithm using both simulated and measured indoor multipath signals, and compared the joint estimation performance of the proposed algorithm with several popular algorithms.\n\nThe remainder of the paper is structured as follows: Section 2 presents the signal model for the SIMO sounding system and introduces the Bernoulli–Gaussian hierarchical prior model for VBI. Section 3 derives the expressions for Bernoulli–Gaussian hierarchical prior-based VBI within the SAGE framework. In Section 4, we propose algorithms that enhance estimation accuracy and reduce computational complexity compared to existing methods for the joint estimation of multipath signal parameters. Section 5 compares the estimation performance of our proposed algorithm with that of related algorithms using simulations and indoor-measured multipath signals and the experimental results have been discussed. Finally, Section 6 provides concluding remarks.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBlind identification of multipath channels: A parametric subspace approach | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/3318036_Blind_identification_of_multipath_channels_A_parametric_subspace_approach)\nciteturn649search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; # Blind identification of multipath channels: A parametric subspace approach ... DOI:10.1109/78.928700 ... This paper investigates a complete blind receiver approach in an unknown multipath fading channel, which has multiple tasks including blind channel estimation, noise power estimation, modulation classification, channel coding recognition, and data detection. ... We propose two algorithms based on the sample covariance matrix of the received signal (i.e., second-order statistics) that take advantage of this linear parameterization: a new identification algorithm that estimates the outer product of the model coefficients via multiplication by a predetermined matrix, and a multiuser version of the previously presented “subspace method” that employs knowledge of the transmission pulse. ... In light of the fundamental relationship between the spectral correlation densities (SCD) of channel input and output, we apply some simple techniques of harmonic retrieval, outlier censoring, and combinatorial analysis to determine the number of paths and estimate the parameters, i.e., the path delays and attenuation For different baseband pulse shapes such as rectangular and raise-cosine pulses, some practical discussions on the channel identifiability and resolution performance of the algorithm are included.\n\nArticle\n\n# Blind identification of multipath channels: A parametric subspace approach\n\n  * August 2001\n  * IEEE Transactions on Signal Processing 49(7):1468 - 1480\n\nDOI:10.1109/78.928700\n\n  * Source\n  * IEEE Xplore\n\nAuthors:\n\nLisa Meilhac\n\nEric Moulines\n\n  * Mines Paris, PSL University\n\nKarim abed-meraim\n\n  * University of Orléans\n\nPascal Chevalier\n\nPascal Chevalier\n\n## No full-text available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\n## Citations (37)\n\n## References (41)\n\n... A parametric subspace approach using the second-order moment was adopted to identify the specular multipath propagation channels in [8]. The proposed parametric method can estimate the channel parameters including attenuations, relative delays, and spatial signatures, which are robust to the channel order overestimation compared to the classical subspace method. ...\nConference Paper\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Aug 2004\n\n  * Athanasios A. Rontogiannis\n  * Kostas Berberidis\n\nA novel equalization schemes based on parametric channel estimation is presented. The proposed scheme is appropriate for channels with long and sparse impulse response such as encountered in high definition digital video transmission systems. The main trait of the channel estimation part is that instead of seeking the whole channel impulse response (CIR), only the unknown time delays and attenuation factors of the physical channel multipath components are efficiently estimated. The equalization part comprises an adaptive decision feedback equalizer (DFE), whose initialization part is properly designed for sparse channels. The information needed in the initialization part is provided by the channel estimation part. The DFE converges much faster compared to the conventional DFE and the whole scheme utilizes a very short training sequence and hence a significant saving in bandwidth is achieved.\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Feb 1999\n  * Acoust Speech Signal Process\n\n  * Lisa Meilhac\n  * P. Duhamel\n  * Pascal Chevalier\n  * Eric Moulines\n\nMost second order single input multiple output (SIMO) identification algorithms identify the global impulse channel response, convolution of an emission filter and a propagation channel. This paper makes an explicit use of this channel structure in a second order algorithm. We present several structured methods exploiting more or less prior information on the emission filter. Proofs of convergence are provided, and simulations show that some knowledge based algorithms greatly improve over classical blind algorithms, even in the case where the knowledge is partial\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nA deterministic blind identification technique for SIMO systems of unknown model order\n\nConference Paper\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Apr 1999\n  * Acoust Speech Signal Process\n\n  * G.T. Venkatesan\n  * 【118†L. View\n\nShow abstract\n\nA new approach to blind identification and equalization of multipath channels\n\nConference Paper\n\n  * Dec 1991\n\n  * Liuzhu Tong\n  * G. Xu\n  * Thomas Kailath\n\nA novel blind channel identification and equalization method is proposed by exploiting the cyclostationarity of communication signals. Identification and equalization of possibly nonminimum phase multipath channels are achieved without using training signals. Unlike most of the adaptive blind equalization methods for which the convergence properties are often problematic, the eigenstructure-based channel estimation algorithm proposed here is asymptotically exact. Based on the second-order properties, the proposed approach identifies nonminimum phase channels and achieves equalization with fewer samples than most techniques based on higher-order statistics. Simulations demonstrate the promising performance of the proposed algorithm in a blind equalization of a three-ray multipath channel\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDelay-locking: Unraveling Multiple Unknown Signals in Unknown Multipath | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/343411088_Delay-locking_Unraveling_Multiple_Unknown_Signals_in_Unknown_Multipath)\nciteturn649search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Conference Paper\n\n# Delay-locking: Unraveling Multiple Unknown